---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const phases = [
  {
    n: 1,
    name: 'Ask',
    question: 'What is the real problem, and whose is it?',
    output: 'A business question, the decision it unlocks, and metrics with operational definitions.',
  },
  {
    n: 2,
    name: 'Prepare',
    question: 'Is this data any good, and can I trust it?',
    output: 'A record for every source: origin, licence, period, known biases.',
  },
  {
    n: 3,
    name: 'Process',
    question: 'Is it clean, and can I prove it?',
    output: 'A cleaning log where every transformation carries its reason and its row counts.',
  },
  {
    n: 4,
    name: 'Analyse',
    question: 'What does the data actually say?',
    output: 'Results, plus the checks I ran to try to break them.',
  },
  {
    n: 5,
    name: 'Share',
    question: 'Will the audience understand it?',
    output: 'Charts whose headline is the finding, not the variable name.',
  },
  {
    n: 6,
    name: 'Act',
    question: 'What should the business do?',
    output: 'Recommendations ranked by impact against effort, with their limits stated.',
  },
];

const layers = [
  {
    name: 'The card',
    time: '15 seconds',
    what: 'Headline, key chart, and what the case demonstrates. The home page.',
  },
  {
    name: 'The case',
    time: '5 minutes',
    what: 'Context, data, process, findings, recommendations, limitations.',
  },
  {
    name: 'The evidence',
    time: '30 minutes',
    what: 'Cleaning log, scripts, source records, the full case record. In the repository.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description="How I run a data analysis case study: six phases, each with an exit gate that has to be passed before the next one starts."
>
  <article class="shell method">
    <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
    <h1>How I work</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Every case on this site runs through the same six phases — and so does most of my paid work,
      where this process sits behind financial reporting that people make decisions on. What makes
      it a method rather than a habit is that every phase has an exit gate.
    </p>

    <p>
      I don&rsquo;t move to the next phase until every item on that gate is met. When something
      fails, I go back rather than improvise forward — finding out during analysis that the data
      can&rsquo;t answer the question means returning to Ask, not writing a softer conclusion.
    </p>

    <h2>The six phases</h2>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>#</th>
            <th>Phase</th>
            <th>Question it answers</th>
            <th>What it produces</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            phases.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="num">{p.n}</td>
                <td><strong>{p.name}</strong></td>
                <td>{p.question}</td>
                <td>{p.output}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Three rules I don&rsquo;t break</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>I document as I go, never afterwards.</strong> In three of the six phases the
      documentation <em>is</em> the deliverable. Reconstructing decisions from memory produces weak
      work, and it&rsquo;s exactly the detail an experienced reviewer notices.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>I don&rsquo;t invent numbers.</strong> If a figure didn&rsquo;t come out of the data,
      it doesn&rsquo;t enter the report. Where there&rsquo;s a gap, I declare it as a limitation
      instead of papering over it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>I never edit raw data in place.</strong> Every transformation produces a new file and
      an entry in the cleaning log, so the whole chain can be re-run from the original source — by
      me next month, or by you today.
    </p>

    <h2>What you can read, and how deep</h2>

    <p>
      A case study produces far more material than anyone wants to read at once, so I publish it in
      three layers. This page and the case pages are the first two; the third is one click away in
      each case&rsquo;s repository, for anyone who&rsquo;d rather check the work than take my word
      for it.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Layer</th>
            <th>Time</th>
            <th>What it is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            layers.map((l) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{l.name}</strong></td>
                <td class="nowrap">{l.time}</td>
                <td>{l.what}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where the questions come from</h2>

    <p>
      I pick questions I actually want answered. The Steam case started because I wanted to know
      whether cheap games really are worse, or whether that&rsquo;s just something people repeat —
      and the only honest way to settle it was to go and look. That&rsquo;s the first filter: if
      I&rsquo;m not curious enough to argue with my own result, the case won&rsquo;t be worth
      defending to anyone else.
    </p>

    <p>
      The second filter is what a case will teach me. Each one is chosen partly for the tool or
      technique I want to get better at, which is why the next will lean on <strong>SQL</strong>:
      it&rsquo;s what I use daily at work, and nothing here shows it yet.
    </p>

    <p class="lineage">
      The six-phase spine — Ask, Prepare, Process, Analyse, Share, Act — comes from the Google Data
      Analytics process. The exit gates, the three-layer publishing model and the templates behind
      every case are mine, built while running this on real work.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  h1 {
    margin: var(--space-s) 0 var(--space-m);
  }

  /* El texto mantiene su medida; las tablas ocupan todo el ancho del shell,
     que es bastante más. */
  .method > p,
  .method > h2 {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: var(--space-m) var(--space-l);
  }

  .num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* El linaje va al cierre y en voz baja: reconoce de dónde viene el esqueleto
     sin que la página abra sonando a curso. */
  .lineage {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-m);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .nowrap {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
